#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096388 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90455606-1080-552e-a825-0cc7054b4be3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "210f48cc-8812-564d-9dae-5a8a42165ef9" :question/math-academy-id "e-14005" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=-4 \\\\ x-\\frac{y}{6} &=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $6.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=-4 \\\\ x-\\frac{y}{6} &=\\frac{5}{2} && |\\,\\times 6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Let's solve for $x$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & =-4 \\\\ x & =-4-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $x=-4-2y$ into the second equation and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-y & =15 \\\\ 6(-4-2y)-y & =15 \\\\ -24-12y-y & =15 \\\\ -24-13y & =15 \\\\ -13y & =39 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $x,$ we substitute $y=-3$ into the equation $x=-4-2y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-4-2y \\\\  & =-4-2(-3) \\\\  & =-4+6 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 2$ and $y = -3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096389 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096637 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c880ac-41a9-543f-b7a1-b4dfcc5de99d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "ff844be7-7d1e-5a1f-a4d6-1d4313f5fd2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14006" :question/problem "Find the solution to the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5a+\\frac{3b}{2} &=2 \\\\ \\frac{4a}{3}+2b &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions, we multiply the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5a+\\frac{3b}{2} &=2 && |\\,\\times 2 \\\\ \\frac{4a}{3}+2b &=0 && |\\,\\times 3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2,$ then we will get a $-6b$ term that can cancel with the $6b$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}10a+3b &=4 && |\\,\\times (-2) \\\\ 4a+6b &=0 && \\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $b$-variable, and allows us to solve for $a\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-20a-\\cancel{6b} & =-8 \\\\ 4a+\\cancel{6b} & =0 \\\\ (-20a+4a) & =(-8+0) \\\\ -16a & =-8 \\\\ a & =\\frac{-8}{-16} \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $a=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the equation $4a+6b=0$ to solve for $b\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a+6b & =0 \\\\ 4(\\frac{1}{2})+6b & =0 \\\\ 2+6b & =0 \\\\ 6b & =-2 \\\\ b & =-\\frac{2}{6} \\\\ b & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $a = \\dfrac{1}{2}, \\,b =-\\dfrac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096394 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14370371-529b-5f25-9963-62c0cd9a172e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Elimination"}] :question/id #uuid "fb7a4815-45e8-5288-8e96-c3741f1a2097" :question/math-academy-id "e-14732" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x-5y &=-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}x+3y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above." :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $4.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x-5y &=-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}x+3y &=2 && |\\,\\times 4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $-x$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $x$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable, and allows us to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\cancel{x}-5y & =-1 \\\\ \\cancel{x}+12y & =8 \\\\ 7y & =7 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=1$ into the equation $-x-5y=-1$ to solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-5y & =-1 \\\\ -x-5\\cdot 1 & =-1 \\\\ -x-5 & =-1 \\\\ -x & =4 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-4, \\, y=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096395 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7eb3fea-e4cf-5537-9a33-06b9d2b35c6b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations Using Elimination"}] :question/id #uuid "7f77dc95-b22e-51af-a8c2-ef60ae4f3098" :question/math-academy-id "e-14733" :question/problem "Find the solution to the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}x+2y &=3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}x-\\frac{2}{3}y &=-7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $6,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}x+2y &=3 && |\\times 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}x-\\frac{2}{3}y &=-7 && |\\times 6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. We can eliminate $y$ by adding the two equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+\\cancel{4y} & =6 \\\\ x-\\cancel{4y} & =-42 \\\\ (5x+x) & =(6-42) \\\\ 6x & =-36 \\\\ x & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-6$ into the second equation to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-4y & =-42 \\\\ -6-4y & =-42 \\\\ -4y & =-36 \\\\ y & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-6$ and $y=9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90455606-1080-552e-a825-0cc7054b4be3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0deee66f-f1c1-5fc7-973a-d2118b0dde4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0deee66f-f1c1-5fc7-973a-d2118b0dde4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288131} :answer-field/id #uuid "666676b7-4b09-5b00-aaa1-e3cae5f5d335" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289951} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fef28ec2-8d49-5650-9a07-d4d69ec832b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fef28ec2-8d49-5650-9a07-d4d69ec832b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289721} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8fbc86e-8f6d-598c-8e41-15c7fa336d29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4da87082-65b7-55e8-a455-70124fce0262" :question/math-academy-id "q-215738" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2p+4q &=-6 \\\\ \\frac{3p}{2}+\\frac{5q}{2} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,q=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $2.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2p+4q &=-6 \\\\ \\frac{3p}{2}+\\frac{5q}{2} &=2 && |\\,\\times 2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2p+4q &=-6 \\\\ 3p+5q &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Let's solve for $p$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p+4q & =-6 \\\\ 2p & =-4q-6 \\\\ p & =-2q-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $p=-2q-3$ into the second equation and solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p+5q & =4 \\\\ 3(-2q-3)+5q & =4 \\\\ -6q-9+5q & =4 \\\\ -q & =13 \\\\ q & =-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, to find the value of $p,$ we substitute $q=-13$ into the equation $p=-2q-3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =-2q-3 \\\\  & =-2(-13)-3 \\\\  & =26-3 \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=23,\\,q=-13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7eb3fea-e4cf-5537-9a33-06b9d2b35c6b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations Using Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05248fe5-5f7a-5b88-bac4-277d935e8d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=6,\\,b=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288068} {:answer/id #uuid "40732601-d1fe-5462-bcbf-5ac0de185f4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-4,\\,b=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288473} {:answer/id #uuid "5d759700-7377-5b33-a932-dd76dd087e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-8,\\,b=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288684} {:answer/id #uuid "860b4286-7355-5ef0-8cf0-49399f52b04f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=8,\\,b=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288939} {:answer/id #uuid "e616263a-88d6-5509-9955-bcea3d00e6d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-4,\\,b=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d759700-7377-5b33-a932-dd76dd087e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-8,\\,b=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288684} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b7b004d-1ebf-54fc-bed6-9e2d986b4568" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2738428-0a2a-5ef8-946f-188443bb06da" :question/math-academy-id "q-216309" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}a+b &=-3 \\\\ a-\\frac{1}{3}b &=-9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $4$ and the second equation by $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}a+b &=-3 && |\\times 4 \\\\ a-\\frac{1}{3}b &=-9 && |\\times 3\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3a+4b &=-12 \\\\ 3a-b &=-27\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-1,$ then we will get a $-3a$ term that can cancel with the $3a$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3a+4b &=-12 && |\\,\\times (-1) \\\\ 3a-b &=-27 && \\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3a-4b &=12 \\\\ 3a-b &=-27\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $a$-variable, and allows us to solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\cancel{3a}-4b & =12 \\\\ \\cancel{3a}-b & =-27 \\\\ (-4b-b) & =(12-27) \\\\ -5b & =-15 \\\\ b & =\\frac{-15}{-5} \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $b=3$ into the second equation to solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a-b & =-27 \\\\ 3a-3 & =-27 \\\\ 3a & =-24 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{24}{3} \\\\ a & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $a=-8,\\,b=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14370371-529b-5f25-9963-62c0cd9a172e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b323228e-9426-58d2-9640-2e8b534088f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p =-1, q = 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095839} {:answer/id #uuid "b57485ed-818c-5a23-b78f-d34b4bcb3ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p =-5, q = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095840} {:answer/id #uuid "3447a2b7-461a-56d5-bfd6-db6f0b101be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p = 5, q = 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095841} {:answer/id #uuid "416128ab-697d-5e0b-86cf-29f393c1181c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p = 4, q = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095842} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf3024c-ea07-5c8c-a3c5-baa897b53fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p = 3, q = 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095843}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6ba83247-057c-59a6-9333-3de8fa7c2437" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095838}] :question/id #uuid "075ffa39-e6dc-5458-b079-fafe8364cbd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-52198" :question/problem "${\\frac{2}{5}p + q = 1; 